<!--
  Inspection SETTINGS that the YAML profile cannot express. Enablement, severity and path scoping all stay
  in the profile beside this directory - code-quality-config/java/qodana/profiles/java.yaml, as a consuming
  project sees it - and this file carries only option VALUES, and only the ones whose type the YAML format
  has no syntax for. The gate copies it, with the rest of ../overrides, into the scan's working directory
  (.qodana/idea-config) and mounts THAT over `/data/project/.idea`, so the developer's own `.idea/` is never
  read or written.

  THE CONTRACT IS THE DIRECTORY AND `myName` - NOT THIS FILE'S NAME. Unlike ../misc.xml, where the filename
  is what the IDE looks up, project inspection settings are found by scanning `.idea/inspectionProfiles/`,
  so this file may be called anything. Measured: renamed from `Project_Default.xml` to the current name,
  the override still applied in full. It does have to be IN that directory, though - measured, moved up to
  sit beside ../misc.xml it is never read at all, and the findings simply come back. What IS load-bearing
  besides the directory is the profile name below - `Project Default` is
  the project profile Qodana resolves, and measured, changing it to anything else drops this file on the
  floor: the annotations vanish from the effective profile and the findings come back, with no error and no
  warning. Do not "tidy" `myName` to match the filename.

  WHY IT IS NEEDED. Qodana types the YAML `options:` block as Map<String, String>, so a set-valued option is
  unreachable from it. Both spellings were measured: a YAML list aborts the run in 9s with
  "Cannot deserialize value of type java.lang.String from Array value", and a comma-joined string is
  accepted and then SILENTLY DROPPED - the run completes, the findings stay, and nothing anywhere says why.
  The reason is structural: a set is serialised as CHILD ELEMENTS (see the shape below), while the YAML
  block can only produce a `value="..."` attribute, so no separator could ever have worked.

  IT IS NOT THE ENTRY-POINTS LIST. `EntryPointsManager` in ../misc.xml answers "is this declaration
  reachable", which is what the dead-code family needs. `InterfaceNeverImplemented` asks a different
  question - "does a concrete class implement this interface" - and a build-time-generated implementation is
  not in the PSI however reachable the interface is. Measured: `io.smallrye.config.ConfigMapping` has been
  in that entry-point list all along and the interfaces were reported anyway.

  THE SHAPE IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. `option` -> `value` -> `item value="..."` is exactly what
  ExternalizableStringSet.writeSettings emits and readExternal expects (it reads getChildren("item") and
  each item's "value" attribute). A `<list>`, an `<option value=...>` child or an attribute on `<value>`
  reads back as the empty set - again silently.

  VERIFY A CHANGE HERE, DO NOT ASSUME IT TOOK. Every failure mode above is quiet, so check the profile the
  scan actually ran with rather than the finding count alone:
      grep -c 'io.smallrye.config.ConfigMapping' .qodana/results/log/effective.profile.xml   # 0 = ignored
  A working override appears once per scope plus once at tool level (20 with the current scope set), and
  leaves everything else untouched - 811 enabled tools and zero `unused` findings, before and after.

  ONE FILE FOR ALL OF THEM. Only one project profile is ever active, so a second file here would not add a
  second set of overrides - any future option that the YAML cannot carry belongs in this same profile,
  alongside the tool below. That is why the file is named for what it holds rather than for one inspection.

  WHAT THE ENTRIES BUY. Every @ConfigMapping interface is implemented by SmallRye at build time, and a
  @FunctionalInterface by a lambda (or, for GitHubReleasesApi, by the REST client built at runtime), so no
  concrete implementor exists in the source. Without this, 4 of the 12 config interfaces were reported -
  and only 4 because the other 9 happen to have test stubs, so deleting a stub would have failed the gate
  on an interface nobody had touched.
-->
<component name="InspectionProjectProfileManager">
  <profile version="1.0">
    <option name="myName" value="Project Default" />
    <inspection_tool class="InterfaceNeverImplemented" enabled="true" level="WARNING" enabled_by_default="true">
      <option name="ignorableAnnotations">
        <value>
          <item value="io.smallrye.config.ConfigMapping" />
          <item value="java.lang.FunctionalInterface" />
        </value>
      </option>
      <option name="ignoreInterfacesThatOnlyDeclareConstants" value="false" />
    </inspection_tool>
  </profile>
</component>
